arXiv:2509.03475v1 [math.OC] 03 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hong Ye Tan  </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">hyt35@cam.ac.uk</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">University of Cambridge</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subhadip Mukherjee  </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">smukherjee@ece.iitkgp.ac.in</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">IIT Kharagpur</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Junqi Tang  </span><span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">j.tang.2@bham.ac.uk</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">University of Birmingham</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Inverse problems lie at the heart of modern imaging science, with broad applications in areas such as medical imaging, remote sensing, and microscopy. Recent years have witnessed a paradigm shift in solving imaging inverse problems, where data-driven regularizers are used increasingly, leading to remarkably high-fidelity reconstruction. A particularly notable approach for data-driven regularization is to use learned image denoisers as implicit priors in iterative image reconstruction algorithms. This survey presents a comprehensive overview of this powerful and emerging class of algorithms, commonly referred to as plug-and-play (PnP) methods. We begin by providing a brief background on image denoising and inverse problems, followed by a short review of traditional regularization strategies. We then explore how proximal splitting algorithms, such as the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM) and proximal gradient descent (PGD), can naturally accommodate learned denoisers in place of proximal operators, and under what conditions such replacements preserve convergence. The role of Tweedie’s formula in connecting optimal Gaussian denoisers and score estimation is discussed, which lays the foundation for regularization-by-denoising (RED) and more recent diffusion-based posterior sampling methods. We discuss theoretical advances regarding the convergence of PnP algorithms, both within the RED and proximal settings, emphasizing the structural assumptions that the denoiser must satisfy for convergence, such as non-expansiveness, Lipschitz continuity, and local homogeneity. We also address practical considerations in algorithm design, including choices of denoiser architecture and acceleration strategies. By integrating both classical optimization insights and modern learning-based priors, this survey aims to provide a unified and accessible framework for understanding PnP methods and their theoretical underpinnings.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inverse problems are the backbone of numerous applications in imaging science, signal processing, computational physics, and beyond. In a typical <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ill-posed</span> imaging inverse problem, one seeks to recover an unknown signal or image <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from its indirect and often noisy observations <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=Kx+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=Kx+w</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the forward operator representing the imaging process and <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> denotes measurement noise. The image <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its measurement <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to lie in appropriate normed vector spaces. Classic examples include low-level computer vision tasks, such as image deblurring, denoising, super-resolution, and inpainting, as well as various medical imaging tasks, including MRI, PET, and CT reconstruction. Due to incomplete or corrupted measurements, inverse problems are fundamentally ill-posed and require <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">regularization</span> to ensure stable and meaningful solutions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional approaches to regularization (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> for a detailed treatment) rely on the design of explicit prior models that encode different regularity assumptions on images, such as Tikhonov regularization, total variation (TV), or, more recently, sparsity-promoting regularizers (typically penalizing the <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm in a suitable transform domain). These classical regularization approaches, while theoretically grounded and computationally tractable, often fail to fully capture the rich and highly structured nature of natural images, thereby limiting reconstruction quality in challenging scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thanks to the pioneering work by Venkatakrishnan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>, <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plug-and-play</em> (PnP) methods have emerged in the last decade as an effective paradigm that deviates from handcrafted priors by integrating powerful image denoisers directly into iterative optimization algorithms for solving inverse problems (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> for a recent survey on PnP methods). The key insight behind PnP is deceptively simple yet quite powerful: instead of specifying an explicit prior, one can “plug in” an off-the-shelf image denoiser within an iterative framework such as <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alternating direction method of multipliers</span> (ADMM) or <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proximal gradient descent</span> (PGD), effectively regularizing the solution through repeated denoising operations. This idea has led to a family of flexible algorithms, with PnP-ADMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and PnP-PGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> being two of the most prominent and widely studied variants of PnP algorithms. PnP methods are particularly attractive due to their modularity; the same image denoiser leads to a reasonable image reconstruction for different forward operators. By decoupling the forward model from the prior, PnP enables practitioners to exploit state-of-the-art denoisers, whether model-based (such as BM3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>) or data-driven (e.g. deep convolutional neural network (CNN)-based denoisers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>, <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite>), without the need to derive complex closed-form priors or incorporate explicit regularizers into the variational framework. This practical plug-and-play nature makes these methods highly attractive and flexible for diverse applications where noise statistics, measurement models, and image features vary widely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In parallel, the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regularization by denoising</em> (RED) approach  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> offers another compelling framework by constructing a class of explicit regularizers using off-the-shelf image denoisers. RED provides a unifying view that connects variational principles with denoising-based operators, offering fixed-point iterations with interpretability and, under suitable conditions, convergence guarantees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>. Other notable theoretical advances include rigorous convergence guarantees for PnP-PGD and PnP-ADMM under appropriate Lipschitz continuity assumptions on the denoising residual (see Theorems 1, 2, and Corollary 3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>), and the extension of PnP concepts to broader classes of iterative schemes, such as consensus equilibrium <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and block coordinate methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>. The synergy between PnP frameworks and deep learning has further amplified their impact. Learned denoisers, such as DnCNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>, FFDNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite>, and more recent transformer-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> or denoising diffusion model-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a>]</cite> can be seamlessly integrated into PnP schemes, delivering remarkable reconstruction performance even in severely ill-posed settings. Beyond static images, PnP methods are increasingly being adapted to dynamic and multimodal imaging tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite>, where additional temporal or cross-modality constraints may be incorporated, while still retaining the modular plug-and-play philosophy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the emergence of generative models, particularly diffusion probabilistic models, has opened new directions for solving inverse problems (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> for a recent survey, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> for applications in medical imaging), especially using PnP-like approaches. These models provide powerful priors that can sample high-quality images conditioned on measurements, blending generative sampling with the PnP idea to tackle complex, high-dimensional inverse problems with improved uncertainty quantification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their empirical success, PnP methods still present open challenges. Key questions include understanding their theoretical guarantees when using highly nonlinear or non-expansive denoisers, designing adaptive schemes that can select or learn denoising strength on the fly, and extending the framework to handle non-Gaussian noise, physics-based constraints, or multimodal data fusion. In this survey, we aim to provide a comprehensive and up-to-date account of plug-and-play methods for imaging inverse problems. We trace the historical development of this framework, highlight the underlying theoretical foundations, and discuss advances in algorithmic design and learning-based denoisers. We also discuss the application of PnP denoisers for Bayesian imaging that incorporates denoisers with diffusion-based posterior sampling, and identify open research directions that may shape the future of PnP-based inverse problem solving. We focus particularly on methods that are widely regarded as pioneering in the area of PnP imaging and methods that come with rigorous convergence guarantees.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Brief Survey of Image Denoising</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Image denoising has long been a fundamental problem in signal and image processing, driving the development of increasingly sophisticated models and algorithms over the past several decades. We refer interested readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> for a review on image denoisers, covering classical techniques to modern deep learning-based approaches. In the discussion that follows, we will assume that the image <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is discretized, and can therefore be represented as a vector in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming that the image has <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pixels. Under an additive noise model, the goal of image denoising is to recover an unknown clean image <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from a noisy observation <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=x+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=x+w</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> represents additive noise. Depending on the application, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> may follow a Gaussian or non-Gaussian distribution, and may be white or colored. The fundamental challenge arises from the fact that noise corrupts both low- and high-frequency components in the image, making it difficult to separate noise from fine image details. Designing an effective denoising algorithm therefore requires balancing noise suppression with the preservation of edges, textures, and small structures in the underlying image. An important feature of well-designed (though not necessarily perfect) denoisers is that they can naturally generate a multiscale decomposition of an image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, while still allowing exact reconstruction. This idea parallels classical multiscale decompositions, such as the Laplacian pyramid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, but is achieved here through the action of the denoiser.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The earliest and perhaps most intuitive family of denoising algorithms is based on linear filtering (in spatial or frequency domains) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, Chapters 3, 4)</cite>. Classical linear filters, such as the Gaussian filter, exploit the assumption that noise essentially has largely high-frequency components, while natural images exhibit local spatial smoothness (which represents low-frequency features). These filters convolve the noisy image with a spatially localized kernel, attenuating high-frequency components. Mathematically, the output can be expressed as <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=Hz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=Hz</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the convolution operator defined by a filter kernel. Although simple and computationally efficient, linear smoothing tends to blur edges and oversmooth textures, leading to the loss of critical image details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome the limitations of purely local and linear methods, early advances focused on transform-domain denoising techniques, focusing particularly on transforms that admit a sparse representation of the image. The wavelet transform provides a multi-resolution representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, well-suited for modeling the piecewise smooth nature of natural images. The wavelet shrinkage framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> models noise attenuation by thresholding the wavelet coefficients. If <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an orthonormal wavelet transform and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=Wz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=Wz</annotation></semantics></math> are the noisy coefficients, then a typical wavelet soft-thresholding scheme estimates the clean coefficients as <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{i}=\text{sign}(u_{i})\cdot\max(|u_{i}|-\tau,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{i}=\text{sign}(u_{i})\cdot\max(|u_{i}|-\tau,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a threshold chosen to balance noise removal and detail preservation. The denoised image is obtained by applying the inverse wavelet transform to the thresholded coefficients. Variants such as soft- and hard-thresholding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, as well as Bayesian thresholding rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, have been developed to adapt thresholds to local statistics, improving performance under varying noise levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the success of transform-based methods, they struggle to fully exploit the inherent spatially repeating structures common in natural images. This limitation motivated the development of non-local algorithms that explicitly model self-similarity. A landmark example is the Non-Local Means (NLM) algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which estimates each pixel as a weighted average of pixels across the entire image, with weights determined by the similarity between local neighborhoods. Formally, the estimate for a pixel at location <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{i}=\sum_{j}w_{ij}z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{i}=\sum_{j}w_{ij}z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ij}=\frac{1}{S_{i}}\exp\left(-\frac{\|P_{i}-P_{j}\|_{2}^{2}}{h^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ij}=\frac{1}{S_{i}}\exp\left(-\frac{\|P_{i}-P_{j}\|_{2}^{2}}{h^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are patches centered at pixels <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. The parameter <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> controls the decay of the similarity function, and <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normalizing constant ensuring that the weights sum to one. By exploiting repeated textures and patterns, NLM preserves fine structures that local or transform-based methods often miss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the concept of non-local self-similarity, block-matching and collaborative filtering approaches emerged, with BM3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> becoming one of the most influential practical algorithms for image denoising. BM3D extends the non-local means principle by grouping similar patches into 3D stacks, applying collaborative transform-domain filtering within each group, and aggregating the estimates back to the image domain. The procedure involves block matching, a 3D linear transform (typically a combination of wavelet and discrete cosine transforms), hard-thresholding or Wiener filtering, and inverse transformation. This collaborative filtering step effectively separates signal and noise in the transform domain, and the aggregation of overlapping patches helps to reduce artifacts and improve robustness against mismatches in patch grouping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In parallel, variational methods have provided a rigorous mathematical framework for denoising. Total Variation (TV) regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> is a classic example formulated as <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=\arg\min_{x}\frac{1}{2}\|y-x\|_{2}^{2}+\lambda\|\nabla x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=\arg\min_{x}\frac{1}{2}\|y-x\|_{2}^{2}+\lambda\|\nabla x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the discrete image gradient and the <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm promotes sparsity in the gradient domain. TV denoising preserves edges by favoring piecewise constant regions while suppressing small oscillations due to noise. Despite its edge-preserving properties, TV regularization can suffer from the well-known staircasing effect, where smooth intensity transitions are replaced by piecewise flat regions. To address this, higher-order regularization models and non-convex penalties have been proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite>, providing more flexibility in capturing image textures and fine details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As large datasets and increased computational resources became available, data-driven denoisers emerged as a dominant approach. Early learning-based methods focused on dictionary learning and sparse coding, where an overcomplete dictionary <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is learned from (possibly noisy) image patches. Given a set of noisy patches <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>, extracted from an image, their corresponding sparse codes <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are estimated by solving</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(B,\left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\right)_{i=1}^{p}\right)=\arg\min_{B,\left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\right)_{i=1}^{p}}\sum_{i=1}^{p}\left[\|z_{i}-B\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\|_{2}^{2}+\lambda_{i}\|\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\|_{1}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(B,\left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\right)_{i=1}^{p}\right)=\arg\min_{B,\left(\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\right)_{i=1}^{p}}\sum_{i=1}^{p}\left[\|z_{i}-B\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\|_{2}^{2}+\lambda_{i}\|\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}\|_{1}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">and the denoised patches are reconstructed as <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The K-SVD algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite> is a notable example of this paradigm, providing a flexible and interpretable model that adapts to local structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of deep learning, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have become the de facto standard for state-of-the-art image denoising. CNN-based models exploit large-scale training data to learn highly expressive and powerful mappings from noisy to clean images. A representative architecture, such as DnCNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>, employs multiple convolutional layers, batch normalization, and residual learning to directly estimate the noise component, which is then subtracted from the noisy input. The learned mapping can be described as <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=z-f_{\theta}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=z-f_{\theta}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the denoising function parameterized by the learnable network weights <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Residual learning accelerates convergence and stabilizes training by focusing the network on learning the noise distribution rather than the clean signal itself. Such models achieve remarkable generalization performance across a wide range of noise levels, structures, and image content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances have further enhanced deep learning-based denoisers through the incorporation of attention mechanisms and transformer architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite>, which capture long-range dependencies more effectively than purely local convolutions. These models can model non-local interactions within an image at a global scale, leading to improved reconstruction of repetitive patterns and structures. Moreover, self-supervised denoising approaches have gained significant traction, especially in applications where clean ground truth images are difficult or impossible to obtain. Methods such as Noise2Noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> and Noise2Void <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> exploit the statistical independence of noise realizations or employ blind-spot training to learn denoisers directly from noisy data, broadening the practical applicability of learning-based denoising. Stein’s unbiased risk estimation (SURE) approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> and equivariant denoising <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> offer two attractive frameworks for self-supervised image denoising. While SURE replaces the mean squared error (MSE) with an unbiased estimate of it to eliminate the dependence on reference ground-truth images, the equivariance-based approach exploits rotational (or other) symmetries of images to achieve self-supervised learning of denoisers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The rise of generative models again provides new perspectives for image denoising. Denoising diffusion probabilistic models (DDPMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> represent one of the most promising directions. These models learn to reverse a forward diffusion process (represented through a stochastic differential equation (SDE)) that gradually adds noise to a clean image, enabling the generation of high-fidelity samples through a sequence of denoising steps. The iterative nature of diffusion models aligns naturally with the iterative refinement inherent in many classical denoising algorithms, making them a compelling candidate for plug-and-play priors in more complicated inverse problem settings. Although computationally intensive, diffusion-based denoisers have demonstrated state-of-the-art results and offer principled uncertainty quantification for the recovered image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout these decades of progress, one recurring theme has been the interplay between model-based image priors and data-driven learning of denoisers. Classical algorithms offer interpretability, well-defined mathematical properties, and provable convergence guarantees, but often lack the representational power needed to capture the complexity of natural images. Learned denoisers excel at modeling rich high-dimensional distributions but raise questions about stability, generalization, and robustness under distribution shifts. This tension has inspired the design of hybrid approaches: These methods embed powerful learned denoisers into model-based optimization frameworks. This idea gave rise to the family of plug-and-play (PnP) methods, which have become highly successful because they bring together two advantages: the flexibility and performance of learned denoisers, and the interpretability and control offered by classical iterative schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the evolution of image denoising algorithms reflects a remarkable trajectory, from simple linear filters to sophisticated non-local, transform-domain, variational, and deep learning-based methods. Each generation has expanded our understanding of natural image statistics and improved our ability to suppress noise while preserving important details in the image. These advances have laid the groundwork for modern inverse problem frameworks that leverage powerful denoising priors as modular components. As generative modeling and self-supervised learning continue to mature, they promise to inspire the next wave of innovation in imaging inverse problems and beyond.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Inverse Problems and Regularization</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study of regularization methods for ill-posed inverse problems in imaging originates from Hadamard’s notion of well-posed problems, in the sense that the solution must exist, be unique, and vary continuously with respect to the observed data. The canonical linear inverse problem seeks to recover an image <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> from noisy measurements <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> related through the forward model:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=Kx+w," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=Kx+w,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact operator between Hilbert spaces, and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> represents measurement noise. The ill-posed nature manifests through three distinct aspects of the operator-theoretic framework: either <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">injectivity</span> or <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">surjectivity</span> of the forward operator may not hold, or <span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stability</span> of the solution map might be violated. For instance, if <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact operator with an infinite-dimensional range, then surjectivity and stability are violated. This is, for instance, the case for the ray transform operator that underlies many medical imaging modalities, such as computed tomography (CT) and positron emission tomography (PET). To see this, first consider the case where the range <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> of the forward operator <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is not closed, implying that solutions may not exist for an arbitrary <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, as any non-zero noise component <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> renders the problem unsolvable in the strict sense. Second, the potential non-triviality of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{N}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{N}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>, the null-space of the forward operator <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, violates uniqueness, particularly evident in limited-angle tomography where certain features become invisible. Most critically, the unboundedness of the generalized inverse <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>, when restricted to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{R}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{R}(K)</annotation></semantics></math>, leads to extreme sensitivity to noise, leading to an unstable solution (i.e., a small amount of noise in the measurement yields a drastically different solution).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These challenges become explicit through the singular value decomposition of the compact operator <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\sigma_{m}\langle\cdot,u_{m}\rangle v_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\sigma_{m}\langle\cdot,u_{m}\rangle v_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the asymptotically vanishing singular values <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> cause the naive solution, given by</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}=\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{\langle y,v_{m}\rangle}{\sigma_{m}}u_{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}=\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{\langle y,v_{m}\rangle}{\sigma_{m}}u_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">cause noise components corresponding to small singular values to be catastrophically amplified. Regularization theory addresses this instability by constructing families of approximate solutions <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> through variational formulations:</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\alpha}=\argmin_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\frac{1}{2}\|Kx-y\|_{\mathcal{Y}}^{2}+\alpha R(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Kx</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\alpha}=\argmin_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\frac{1}{2}\|Kx-y\|_{\mathcal{Y}}^{2}+\alpha R(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the functional <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes prior knowledge about plausible solutions. The classical Tikhonov regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite> employs <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)=\|x\|_{\mathcal{X}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)=\|x\|_{\mathcal{X}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the explicit solution <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\alpha}=(K^{*}K+\alpha I)^{-1}K^{*}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\alpha}=(K^{*}K+\alpha I)^{-1}K^{*}y</annotation></semantics></math>,
which corresponds to a spectral filter with coefficients <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\alpha}(\sigma)=\sigma/(\sigma^{2}+\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\alpha}(\sigma)=\sigma/(\sigma^{2}+\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. While the quadratic (<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) penalty on <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees stability of the reconstruction, it does so by uniformly penalizing deviations across all frequencies. This effect can be seen from the Euler–Lagrange optimality condition <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}(Kx-y)+\alpha x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}(Kx-y)+\alpha x=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where the regularization parameter <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a frequency-independent damping term. The outcome is a systematic suppression of both noise and fine-scale features, resulting in overly smoothed reconstructions and noticeable loss of sharp edge structures. To mitigate this limitation, one may instead consider sparsity-promoting penalties such as the <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>. Unlike the quadratic case, the <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm is non-differentiable at zero, which effectively allows the reconstruction to tolerate large local variations and thus preserve sharp discontinuities such as edges. However, this comes with new drawbacks: the piecewise-constant bias induced by <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization can lead to the well-known <span id="S1.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">staircasing artifact</span>, where smooth gradients in the original image are replaced by artificial flat regions separated by sharp transitions of intensity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Moreover, textures and fine oscillatory details tend to be lost, since they are not easily represented in the sparse model enforced by the <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Modern approaches combine multiple regularization functionals through formulations of the form <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\alpha_{l}R_{l}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\alpha_{l}R_{l}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where typical components include sparsity-promoting terms <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Psi x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Psi x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in learned dictionaries, higher-order derivatives <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla^{2}x\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla^{2}x\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and nonlocal operators capturing long-range image dependencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One of the most significant theoretical advancements in recent years has been the development of learned regularization through the plug-and-play framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>, where advanced denoising operators <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> are interpreted as proximal operators corresponding to implicit regularizers:</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma}(x)\approx\prox_{\sigma^{2}R}(x)=\argmin_{z}\frac{1}{2}\|z-x\|^{2}+\sigma^{2}R(z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma}(x)\approx\prox_{\sigma^{2}R}(x)=\argmin_{z}\frac{1}{2}\|z-x\|^{2}+\sigma^{2}R(z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This interpretation leads to provably convergent algorithms (at least in the sense of fixed-point convergence) when the denoiser satisfies appropriate non-expansiveness conditions. The resulting methods combine the theoretical foundations of variational regularization with the excellent empirical performance of data-driven image priors induced by denoisers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Current theoretical challenges include the rigorous characterization of the implicit regularizers associated with modern denoising architectures, the extension to nonlinear forward models, and the development of convergence rates under weaker assumptions on the denoising operators. These questions represent active areas of research at the intersection of functional analysis, optimization theory, and statistical learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Convergent Regularization</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain stable solutions to inverse problems, a mechanism is needed to handle varying noise levels in the measurement. For this purpose, the concept of convergent regularization has proven highly useful. Regularization can be roughly understood as a convergence requirement to a unique solution, such as the minimum-norm solution <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>, where convergence occurs as the noise level <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, consider the previously discussed reconstruction operator <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\mathcal{R}(\cdot,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\alpha}\coloneqq\mathcal{R}(\cdot,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, which parametrizes a family of continuous operators <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\alpha}:\mathcal{Y}\to\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\alpha}:\mathcal{Y}\to\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameter <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the noise level <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|y^{\delta}-y^{0}\|\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|y^{\delta}-y^{0}\|\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{0}:=Kx^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{0}:=Kx^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes noise-free measurement data. We say that the family of reconstruction operators is a <span id="S1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">convergent regularization</span> method if there exists a parameter choice rule <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> such that reconstructions <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\delta}\coloneqq\mathcal{R}_{\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})}(y^{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\delta}\coloneqq\mathcal{R}_{\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})}(y^{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> converge to <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}\coloneqq A^{\dagger}y^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}\coloneqq A^{\dagger}y^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (given by the pseudo-inverse) as noise vanishes, in the sense that</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\limsup_{\delta\to 0}\left\|x^{\delta}-x^{\dagger}\right\|_{\mathcal{X}}=0\quad\text{as}\quad\limsup_{\delta\to 0}\,\{\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})\}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>as</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{\delta\to 0}\left\|x^{\delta}-x^{\dagger}\right\|_{\mathcal{X}}=0\quad\text{as}\quad\limsup_{\delta\to 0}\,\{\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})\}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, we have point-wise convergence of the reconstruction operators to the pseudo-inverse, i.e. <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})}(y^{\delta})\to A^{\dagger}y^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\alpha(\delta,y^{\delta})}(y^{\delta})\to A^{\dagger}y^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer interested readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> for a detailed discussion on convergent regularization schemes (and several convergence rate results in the classical regularization literature). While this is somewhat restrictive as it only considers convergence to the least-squares minimum-norm solution, this nevertheless serves as an important tool to design learned regularization methods, i.e., learned reconstruction approaches that formally satisfy the above convergence criterion.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proximal Splitting Algorithms</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The heart of PnP lies within monotone operator theory, particularly operator splitting. Informally, splitting methods solve a composite optimization problem using simpler gradient-like operations, interpreted as data fidelity steps and regularization steps within the PnP framework. We will introduce in this section the notion of proximal operators and their centrality in convex analysis, and demonstrate how convergence results in monotone operator theory relate to composite convex optimization and further to convergence of PnP methods. For a more in-depth exposition on convex analysis and monotone operator theory, we refer interested readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Foundations of Proximal Calculus</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proximal operator, introduced by Moreau in 1962, serves as the cornerstone of modern nonsmooth optimization. Given a proper, closed, and convex function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, its proximal operator is defined through the solution of the following variational problem:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\prox}_{\lambda f}(v)=\argmin_{x}\left(f(x)+\frac{1}{2\lambda}\|x-v\|_{2}^{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\prox}_{\lambda f}(v)=\argmin_{x}\left(f(x)+\frac{1}{2\lambda}\|x-v\|_{2}^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the case where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable, the proximal operator may be interpreted as a backwards Euler discretization of gradient flow <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}(t)=-\nabla f(x(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}(t)=-\nabla f(x(t))</annotation></semantics></math>. With step size <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, this is given by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{k+1}=x_{k}-\eta\nabla f(x_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=x_{k}-\eta\nabla f(x_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is precisely the first-order optimality condition for <a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The proximal operator generalizes this concept to nonsmooth and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>-valued functions. Furthermore, the proximal operator is a generalization of projection onto a convex set. Suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\chi_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\chi_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is the characteristic function of a convex set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, taking values <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Then, the proximal operator <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{\lambda f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{\lambda f}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the (Euclidean) projection onto <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. This equivalence is useful when interpreting constrained optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a class of general convex functions, the proximal operator has several useful functional properties, as stated in the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a proper closed convex function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, the proximal operator is well-defined and is single-valued. Moreover, it satisfies the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>prox</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{f}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is non-expansive (i.e., 1-Lipschitz) and continuous.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fixed points of </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>prox</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{f}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> correspond to minimizers of </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}^{n}}\mid x_{0}=\prox_{f}(x_{0})\}=\argmin_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}f(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}^{n}}\mid x_{0}=\prox_{f}(x_{0})\}=\argmin_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}f(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Moreau’s identity) </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{f}+\prox_{f^{*}}=\id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{f}+\prox_{f^{*}}=\id</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Id</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\id</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the identity map on </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1 </span>Monotone Operators</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to its functional properties, the proximal operator’s importance to imaging stems from its deep connection to the calculus of subgradients, which can be understood through the lens of monotone operators. In what follows, we give a brief review of monotone operators, paving the way for discussing splitting algorithms that underlie the modern PnP methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Monotonicity)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A set-valued mapping <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightrightarrows\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightrightarrows\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{monotone}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{monotone}</annotation></semantics></math> if for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\,p\in T(x),\,p^{\prime}\in T(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\,p\in T(x),\,p^{\prime}\in T(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p-p^{\prime},x-x^{\prime}\rangle\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p-p^{\prime},x-x^{\prime}\rangle\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <em id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">strictly monotone</em> if the inequality is strict for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resolvent</em> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the operator <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{T}\coloneqq(\id+T)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Id</mi><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{T}\coloneqq(\id+T)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">reflected resolvent</em> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{T}\coloneqq 2J_{T}-\id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{T}\coloneqq 2J_{T}-\id</annotation></semantics></math>.
A set-valued mapping <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">maximally monotone</em> if its graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma(T):=\{(x,p):x\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\,p\in T(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(T):=\{(x,p):x\in\mathbb{R}^{n},\,p\in T(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained within the graph of another monotone operator.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a (proper and closed) convex function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, the subdifferential operator <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial f</annotation></semantics></math> can be seen to be a monotone operator (and indeed, something stronger called cyclically monotone) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>. Analogously to the backward Euler interpretation above, the proximal map can be equivalently characterized as the resolvent of the subdifferential operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, Sec. 12.C.)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{\lambda f}=J_{\partial f}\coloneqq(I+\lambda\,\partial f)^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{\lambda f}=J_{\partial f}\coloneqq(I+\lambda\,\partial f)^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The resolvent is monotone for proper closed functions, and <em id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximally monotone</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is additionally convex. This identity reveals that proximal operations are fundamentally linked to the theory of maximal monotone operators. These theoretical foundations underpin the development of proximal algorithms, particularly the proximal gradient method, arising in convex optimization by leveraging results from monotone operator theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2 </span>Composite Optimization and Operator Splitting</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Common optimization problems encountered in variational image recovery take the composite form <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmin_{x}f(x)+g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmin_{x}f(x)+g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,g</annotation></semantics></math> are proper closed convex functions with possibly different regularity conditions. Considering the first-order optimality conditions, the composite optimization problem is equivalent to the monotone inclusion problem <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in Ax+Bx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in Ax+Bx</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\partial f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\partial f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\partial g</annotation></semantics></math> are both maximally monotone operators, arising naturally while finding a minimizer of the sum of two convex functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For general monotone operators <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, one possible approach is to consider root solving using the resolvent <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\lambda(A+B)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\lambda(A+B)}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this may be difficult to compute. For example, taking <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\partial f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\partial f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\partial g</annotation></semantics></math>, the subgradients of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, this is equivalent to computing the proximal operator of the composite function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+g</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, one seeks to find a zero of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A+B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A+B</annotation></semantics></math>, using only their resolvents <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\lambda A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\lambda A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\lambda B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\lambda B}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is useful in the context of convex problems where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and/or <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> have easily computable proximals, while <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+g</annotation></semantics></math> does not. This process of splitting the resolvent of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A+B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A+B</annotation></semantics></math> into the resolvents of its components is referred to as a <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">splitting algorithm</span> and can be done in different ways <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. We present two simple versions, which are by far the most widely employed splitting techniques in convex optimization: <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proximal gradient descent</span> (sometimes referred to as <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">forward-backward splitting</span> (FBS)) and the <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Douglas–Rachford splitting</span> (DRS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Forward-backward algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Cor. 27.9)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a Hilbert space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-cocoercive (i.e. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x-y,Ax-Ay\rangle\geq\beta\|Bx-By\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x-y,Ax-Ay\rangle\geq\beta\|Bx-By\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) for some <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B:\mathcal{X}\rightrightarrows\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B:\mathcal{X}\rightrightarrows\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be maximally monotone. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,2\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,2\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\min\{1,\,\beta/\lambda\}+1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\min\{1,\,\beta/\lambda\}+1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zer(A+B)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">zer</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zer(A+B)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the forward-backward iterations as</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}y_{n}=x_{n}-\lambda Ax_{n},\\
x_{n+1}=J_{\lambda B}y_{n}-x_{n}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}y_{n}=x_{n}-\lambda Ax_{n},\\
x_{n+1}=J_{\lambda B}y_{n}-x_{n}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The iterates satisfy the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n})_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n})_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converges weakly to a point in </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zer(A+B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">zer</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zer(A+B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\zer(A+B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">zer</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\zer(A+B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ax_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ax_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converges strongly to </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ax</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Douglas–Rachford Splitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Thm. 25.6)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a Hilbert space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B:\mathcal{X}\rightrightarrows\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B:\mathcal{X}\rightrightarrows\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be maximally monotone operators such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zer(A+B)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">zer</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zer(A+B)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a step size and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an initialization. Consider the iterations</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}y=J_{\lambda A}x_{n},\\
z_{n}=J_{\lambda B}(2y-x_{n}),\\
x_{n+1}=x_{n}+z_{n}-y;\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}y=J_{\lambda A}x_{n},\\
z_{n}=J_{\lambda B}(2y-x_{n}),\\
x_{n+1}=x_{n}+z_{n}-y;\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which can be expressed more succinctly as,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{k+1}=J_{\lambda B}(2J_{\lambda A}-I)x^{k}+(I-J_{\lambda A})x^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{k+1}=J_{\lambda B}(2J_{\lambda A}-I)x^{k}+(I-J_{\lambda A})x^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, there exists a fixed point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Fix R_{\lambda B}R_{\lambda A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Fix</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Fix R_{\lambda B}R_{\lambda A}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\lambda A}(x)\in\zer(A+B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">zer</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\lambda A}(x)\in\zer(A+B)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y-z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y-z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converges strongly to zero,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converges weakly to </span><math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converge weakly to </span><math id="S2.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\lambda A}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\lambda A}(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in the case where the Hilbert space <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional, weak convergence is equivalent to strong convergence. To obtain the corresponding convex optimization, simply let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be the subdifferentials of some proper closed convex functions <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. We get convergence to a zero of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{A+B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{A+B}</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalently a fixed point of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{f+g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{f+g}</annotation></semantics></math>, using only proximal operators or subgradients of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> separately. Furthermore, the fixed point is a minimum of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By casting the above monotone inclusion problem in the scope of convex functions, with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> being a derivative and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> being a proximal operator, we can obtain splitting schemes that optimize the sum of two convex functions, where one of the functions is smooth.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.3 </span>PnP Proximal Gradient Descent</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For casting <a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Theorem 2.3 (Forward-backward algorithm ( , , Cor. 27.9)) ‣ 2.1.2 Composite Optimization and Operator Splitting ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> into the context of convex optimization by letting <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\partial g</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the composite optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{x}f(x)+g(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>x</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{x}f(x)+g(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> admits efficient proximal evaluations. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smoothness of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/L</annotation></semantics></math>-cocoercivity of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f</annotation></semantics></math> using the Baillon–Haddad theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The <span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proximal gradient descent</span> (PGD) method generates iterates via:</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda g}\left(x_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(x_{k})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda g}\left(x_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(x_{k})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The convergence properties of this scheme are characterized below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Proximal Gradient Convergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Sec. 10)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth for some <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\geq 0,L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\geq 0,L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be convex. For step size <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,2/L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,2/L)</annotation></semantics></math>, the PGD iterates satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x_{k}-x^{*}\|_{2}\leq\varrho^{k}\|x^{0}-x^{*}\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x_{k}-x^{*}\|_{2}\leq\varrho^{k}\|x^{0}-x^{*}\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho=\max(|1-\lambda L|,|1-\lambda\mu|)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho=\max(|1-\lambda L|,|1-\lambda\mu|)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is merely convex, the objective error decays as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the minimum of the residuals satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{l\leq k}\|x_{l}-x_{l+1}\|=\mathcal{O}(1/k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{l\leq k}\|x_{l}-x_{l+1}\|=\mathcal{O}(1/k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The PnP-PGD method is obtained by replacing the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prox_{\lambda g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prox_{\lambda g}</annotation></semantics></math> term in <a href="#S2.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ 2.1.3 PnP Proximal Gradient Descent ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> with a (Gaussian) denoiser <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard deviation of noise that the denoiser can eliminate:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}\left(x_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(x_{k})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}\left(x_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(x_{k})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(PnP-PGD)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.4 </span>Relaxed PnP Proximal Gradient Descent</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PGD iterations can be relaxed to accommodate for weakly convex functions <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> while minimizing <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+g</annotation></semantics></math>. The relaxed iterations, for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, are <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}q_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k},\\
y_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda g}(y_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(q_{k+1})),\\
x_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k+1}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>prox</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}q_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k},\\
y_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda g}(y_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(q_{k+1})),\\
x_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k+1}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The relaxed PGD algorithm, also known as <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>PGD, enjoys similar convergence results as FBS and DRS. Moreover, the convergence theory can handle weakly convex functions, albeit with a modified objective functional involving an additional residual term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, Thm. 2)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be convex and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex. Then for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&lt;\min((\alpha L_{f})^{-1},\alpha M^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&lt;\min((\alpha L_{f})^{-1},\alpha M^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, define the objective <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\lambda f+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\lambda f+g</annotation></semantics></math>. The iterates <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S2.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 2.1.4 Relaxed PnP Proximal Gradient Descent ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> satisfy</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x_{k})+\frac{\alpha}{2\lambda}\left(1-\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)^{2}\|x_{k}-x_{k+1}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x_{k})+\frac{\alpha}{2\lambda}\left(1-\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)^{2}\|x_{k}-x_{k+1}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is non-increasing and convergent;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The sequence </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has finite length, and </span><math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{l&lt;K}\|x_{l+1}-x_{l}\|=\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{l&lt;K}\|x_{l+1}-x_{l}\|=\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cluster points of the sequence </span><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are stationary points of </span><math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, the gap between the minimum residual rate of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>PGD and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/k)</annotation></semantics></math> for PGD is a consequence of convexity. In PnP methods, we usually deal with weakly convex functions, for which the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math> rate comes from smoothness. Analogous to PnP-PGD, the relaxed PnP-<math id="S2.SS1.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>PGD method arises from replacing the proximal with a denoiser in <a href="#S2.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 2.1.4 Relaxed PnP Proximal Gradient Descent ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}q_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k},\\
y_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(y_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(q_{k+1})),\\
x_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k+1}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}q_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k},\\
y_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(y_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(q_{k+1})),\\
x_{k+1}=(1-\alpha)x_{k}+\alpha y_{k+1}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(PnP-<math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>PGD)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.5 </span>PnP Douglas–Rachford Splitting</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For proper, convex, and closed functions <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable, one can substitute <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\nabla f,B=\partial g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\nabla f,B=\partial g</annotation></semantics></math> into <a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Theorem 2.4 (Douglas–Rachford Splitting ( , , Thm. 25.6)) ‣ 2.1.2 Composite Optimization and Operator Splitting ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> to yield the update</p>
<table id="S2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda g}(2\prox_{\lambda f}-I)x_{k}+(I-\prox_{\lambda f})x_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda g}(2\prox_{\lambda f}-I)x_{k}+(I-\prox_{\lambda f})x_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, Thm. 3.1)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The residuals in the DRS iteration, given by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e_{k}=x_{k}-\prox_{\lambda g}(x_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(x_{k}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{k}=x_{k}-\prox_{\lambda g}(x_{k}-\lambda\nabla f(x_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decay as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|e_{k}\|^{2}=\mathcal{O}(1/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|e_{k}\|^{2}=\mathcal{O}(1/k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The asymmetry of the Douglas–Rachford splitting gives rise to two possible splittings by switching the roles of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. In split form, it gives rise to PnP-DRS and PnP-DRSdiff, defined as follows, where the latter requires that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable for convergence. Notably, the two variants of PnP with the DRS have slightly different convergence assumptions on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The PnP-DRS iterations are given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}y_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(x_{k}),\\
z_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda f}(2y_{k+1}-x_{k}),\\
x_{k+1}=x_{k}+(z_{k+1}-y_{k+1});\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}y_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(x_{k}),\\
z_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda f}(2y_{k+1}-x_{k}),\\
x_{k+1}=x_{k}+(z_{k+1}-y_{k+1});\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(PnP-DRS)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">while the PnP-DRSdiff algorithm generates the following iterates:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}y_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda f}(x_{k}),\\
z_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(2y_{k+1}-x_{k}),\\
x_{k+1}=x_{k}+(z_{k+1}-y_{k+1}).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}y_{k+1}=\prox_{\lambda f}(x_{k}),\\
z_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(2y_{k+1}-x_{k}),\\
x_{k+1}=x_{k}+(z_{k+1}-y_{k+1}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(PnP-DRSdiff)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>ADMM: Constrained Optimization to Operator Splitting</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While PGD and DRS consider composite optimization in one variable, a more general problem may include equality constraints, such as in a Lagrangian. Alternatively, one may be interested in a problem of the form <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)+g(Kx)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)+g(Kx)</annotation></semantics></math> for some forward operator <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with a computable adjoint <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alternating direction method of multipliers</span> (ADMM) algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is equipped to address problems with a separable structure having the general form</p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{x,z}f(x)+g(z)\quad\text{subject to}\quad Kx+Bz=c." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>subject to</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{x,z}f(x)+g(z)\quad\text{subject to}\quad Kx+Bz=c.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">ADMM can be derived from applying DRS to a dual formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite>. To see this, introduce the augmented Lagrangian according to the equality constraint:</p>
<table id="S2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x,z,u)=f(x)+g(z)+u^{\top}(Kx+Bz-c)+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx+Bz-c\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x,z,u)=f(x)+g(z)+u^{\top}(Kx+Bz-c)+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx+Bz-c\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">ADMM then generates iterates through alternating minimization:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}x_{k+1}=\argmin_{x}\,\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x,z_{k},u_{k}),\\
z_{k+1}=\argmin_{z}\,\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x_{k+1},z,u_{k}),\\
u_{k+1}=u_{k}+\rho(Kx_{k+1}+Bz_{k+1}-c).\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}x_{k+1}=\argmin_{x}\,\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x,z_{k},u_{k}),\\
z_{k+1}=\argmin_{z}\,\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x_{k+1},z,u_{k}),\\
u_{k+1}=u_{k}+\rho(Kx_{k+1}+Bz_{k+1}-c).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The connection to Douglas–Rachford splitting emerges when considering the dual problem. We demonstrate this in the special case where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=-I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the primal problem becomes</p>
<table id="S2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{x,z}f(x)+g(z)\quad\text{subject to}\quad Kx=z," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>subject to</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{x,z}f(x)+g(z)\quad\text{subject to}\quad Kx=z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and the corresponding dual problem is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{u}-f^{*}(-K^{*}u)-g^{*}(u)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>u</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{u}-f^{*}(-K^{*}u)-g^{*}(u),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the adjoint of the operator <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalently, the matrix transpose in finite dimensions. The optimality conditions for the dual problem are:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\in-A\partial f^{*}(-K^{*}u^{*})+\partial g^{*}(u^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in-A\partial f^{*}(-K^{*}u^{*})+\partial g^{*}(u^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Updownarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Updownarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exists x^{*},z^{*}\ \text{s.t.}\quad z^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>s.t.</mtext></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exists x^{*},z^{*}\ \text{s.t.}\quad z^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=Kx^{*}\quad\text{where}\quad x^{*}\in\partial f^{*}(-A^{\top}u^{*}),\,z^{*}\in\partial g^{*}(u^{*})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>where</mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Kx^{*}\quad\text{where}\quad x^{*}\in\partial f^{*}(-A^{\top}u^{*}),\,z^{*}\in\partial g^{*}(u^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Define the maximally monotone operators:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{1}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{1}(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-K\partial f^{*}(-K^{*}u)=\partial(f^{*}\circ(-K^{*}))(u)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-K\partial f^{*}(-K^{*}u)=\partial(f^{*}\circ(-K^{*}))(u),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{2}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{2}(u)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\partial g^{*}(u)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\partial g^{*}(u).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.8" class="ltx_p">We need to solve the inclusion problem <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in T_{1}(u)+T_{2}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in T_{1}(u)+T_{2}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using DRS, the iterations may be rewritten as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=J_{\rho T_{1}}(z_{k}+w_{k})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=J_{\rho T_{1}}(z_{k}+w_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=J_{\rho T_{2}}(u_{k+1}-w_{k})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=J_{\rho T_{2}}(u_{k+1}-w_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=w_{k}+z_{k+1}-u_{k+1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=w_{k}+z_{k+1}-u_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.9" class="ltx_p">The resolvent of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> may be recognized exactly as a proximal operator <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J_{\rho T_{2}}=J_{\rho\partial g^{*}}=\prox_{\rho g^{*}}=I-\rho\prox_{\rho^{-1}g}(\rho^{-1}\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\rho T_{2}}=J_{\rho\partial g^{*}}=\prox_{\rho g^{*}}=I-\rho\prox_{\rho^{-1}g}(\rho^{-1}\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. The resolvent of the first operator may also be computed as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{\rho T_{1}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{\rho T_{1}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prox_{\rho f^{*}\circ(-K^{*})}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prox_{\rho f^{*}\circ(-K^{*})}(v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=v-\rho\prox_{\rho^{-1}[f^{*}\circ(-K^{*})]^{*}}(\rho^{-1}v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=v-\rho\prox_{\rho^{-1}[f^{*}\circ(-K^{*})]^{*}}(\rho^{-1}v)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=v-\rho\argmin_{x}\inf_{z,-Az=x}f(z)+\frac{\rho}{2}\|x-\rho^{-1}v\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Az</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=v-\rho\argmin_{x}\inf_{z,-Az=x}f(z)+\frac{\rho}{2}\|x-\rho^{-1}v\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=v+\rho K\argmin_{z}\left(f(z)+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Az+\rho^{-1}v\|^{2}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Az</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=v+\rho K\argmin_{z}\left(f(z)+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Az+\rho^{-1}v\|^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.10" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we have that:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=z+w+\rho K\hat{x}_{1},\quad\text{where}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=z+w+\rho K\hat{x}_{1},\quad\text{where}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{x}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{x}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{1}}\left(f(x_{1})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{1}+\rho^{-1}(z+w)\|^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Kx</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{1}}\left(f(x_{1})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{1}+\rho^{-1}(z+w)\|^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{1}}\left(f(x_{1})+z^{\top}(Kx_{1})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{1}+\rho^{-1}w\|^{2}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Kx</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Kx</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{1}}\left(f(x_{1})+z^{\top}(Kx_{1})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{1}+\rho^{-1}w\|^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.11" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prox_{\rho g^{*}}(u^{+}-w)=\prox_{\rho g^{*}}(z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prox_{\rho g^{*}}(u^{+}-w)=\prox_{\rho g^{*}}(z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1}-\rho\prox_{\rho^{-1}g}(\rho^{-1}(z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1}-\rho\prox_{\rho^{-1}g}(\rho^{-1}(z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=z+\rho(K\hat{x}_{1}-\hat{x}_{2}),\quad\text{where}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=z+\rho(K\hat{x}_{1}-\hat{x}_{2}),\quad\text{where}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{x}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{x}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prox_{\rho^{-1}g}(\rho^{-1}(z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prox_{\rho^{-1}g}(\rho^{-1}(z+\rho K\hat{x}_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{2}}\left(g(x_{2})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|x_{2}-K\hat{x}_{1}-\rho^{-1}z\|^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{2}}\left(g(x_{2})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|x_{2}-K\hat{x}_{1}-\rho^{-1}z\|^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{2}}\left(g(x_{2})-z^{\top}x_{2}+\frac{\rho}{2}\|K\hat{x}_{1}-x_{2}\|^{2}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmin_{x_{2}}\left(g(x_{2})-z^{\top}x_{2}+\frac{\rho}{2}\|K\hat{x}_{1}-x_{2}\|^{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.12" class="ltx_p">Finally, the dual DRS update <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{+}=w+z^{+}-u^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{+}=w+z^{+}-u^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> simplifies to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{+}=-\rho\hat{x}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{+}=-\rho\hat{x}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This may be re-substituted into the expression for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now considering the variables <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{x}_{k,1},\hat{x}_{k,2},z_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{x}_{k,1},\hat{x}_{k,2},z_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, the iteration simplifies to</p>
<table id="S2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\hat{x}_{k+1,1}=\argmin_{x_{1}}\left(f(x_{1})+z_{k}^{\top}(Kx_{1})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{k,1}-\hat{x}_{k,2}\|^{2}\right),\\
\hat{x}_{k+1,2}=\argmin_{x_{2}}\left(g(x_{2})-z_{k}^{\top}x_{2}+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{k+1,1}-\hat{x}_{k,2}\|^{2}\right),\\
z_{k+1}=z_{k}+\rho(K\hat{x}_{k+1,1}-\hat{x}_{k+1,2}).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Kx</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Kx</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Kx</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\hat{x}_{k+1,1}=\argmin_{x_{1}}\left(f(x_{1})+z_{k}^{\top}(Kx_{1})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{k,1}-\hat{x}_{k,2}\|^{2}\right),\\
\hat{x}_{k+1,2}=\argmin_{x_{2}}\left(g(x_{2})-z_{k}^{\top}x_{2}+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{k+1,1}-\hat{x}_{k,2}\|^{2}\right),\\
z_{k+1}=z_{k}+\rho(K\hat{x}_{k+1,1}-\hat{x}_{k+1,2}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.13" class="ltx_p">Recalling the (simplified) form of the augmented Lagrangian</p>
<table id="S2.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x_{1},x_{2},z)=f(x_{1})+g(x_{2})+z^{\top}(Kx_{1}-x_{2})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{1}-x_{2}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\rho}(x_{1},x_{2},z)=f(x_{1})+g(x_{2})+z^{\top}(Kx_{1}-x_{2})+\frac{\rho}{2}\|Kx_{1}-x_{2}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.14" class="ltx_p">We observe that the minimizations are precisely with respect to the first two arguments, exactly yielding the ADMM algorithm. In the context of imaging inverse problems, the PnP variant of the ADMM algorithm emerges by replacing the proximal operator (with respect to a nonsmooth regularizer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>) with an off-the-shelf denoiser <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, while minimizing the variational objective with an <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> fidelity term: <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{x}{\min}\,\frac{1}{2}\|y-Kx\|_{2}^{2}+g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑥</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{x}{\min}\,\frac{1}{2}\|y-Kx\|_{2}^{2}+g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. To solve this problem using ADMM, one applies the variable separation trick to reformulate the problem as</p>
<table id="S2.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{x,z}{\min}\,\frac{1}{2}\|y-Kx\|_{2}^{2}+g(z)\text{\,\,subject to\,\,}x=z." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">z</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> subject to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{x,z}{\min}\,\frac{1}{2}\|y-Kx\|_{2}^{2}+g(z)\text{\,\,subject to\,\,}x=z.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.15" class="ltx_p">Applying (<a href="#S2.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ 2.2 ADMM: Constrained Optimization to Operator Splitting ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) on (<a href="#S2.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ 2.2 ADMM: Constrained Optimization to Operator Splitting ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) and using a denoiser in place of the proximal operator, the PnP-ADMM iterations can be derived as follows, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is an appropriately chosen step size parameter:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}x_{k+1}=\left(K^{*}K+\rho\,I\right)^{-1}\left(K^{*}y+\rho\,(z_{k}-u_{k})\right),\\
z_{k+1}=D\left(x_{k+1}+u_{k}\right),\\
u_{k+1}=u_{k}+\left(x_{k+1}-z_{k+1}\right).\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}x_{k+1}=\left(K^{*}K+\rho\,I\right)^{-1}\left(K^{*}y+\rho\,(z_{k}-u_{k})\right),\\
z_{k+1}=D\left(x_{k+1}+u_{k}\right),\\
u_{k+1}=u_{k}+\left(x_{k+1}-z_{k+1}\right).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(PnP-ADMM)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Accelerated Convex Methods</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the previous reformulation of plug-and-play methods as non-convex optimization of some explicit functionals, a natural question is whether or not the optimization can be accelerated. This has direct implications for the reconstruction speed, which is given by the rate of convergence to critical points of said functionals. There are two main ways of acceleration in the optimization literature, namely via momentum and preconditioning. We note that there are no available convergence guarantees in the former case of momentum-based accelerated PnP, other than spectral analyses for linear denoisers and linear inverse problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>. In gradient-based optimization, preconditioning refers to multiplying the gradient by some (usually positive definite) preconditioner matrix, to make the problem “less ill-conditioned” and achieve faster convergence using larger step sizes. Common instances include Newton’s method, Riemannian gradient descent, or the more exotic mirror descent. In the proximal splitting case, however, the preconditioning affects not only the gradient step but also the proximal step. For example, the preconditioned proximal gradient method to minimize <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+g</annotation></semantics></math> takes the form</p>
<table id="S2.E25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="4"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E25.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E25.1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k+1}=\prox_{g}^{B_{k}}(x-B_{k}^{-1}\nabla f(x_{k}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>prox</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k+1}=\prox_{g}^{B_{k}}(x-B_{k}^{-1}\nabla f(x_{k})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E25.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E25.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\prox_{g}^{B_{k}}(x)=\argmin_{y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}g(y)+\frac{1}{2}(y-x)^{\top}B_{k}(y-x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>prox</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\prox_{g}^{B_{k}}(x)=\argmin_{y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}g(y)+\frac{1}{2}(y-x)^{\top}B_{k}(y-x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For Newton-type acceleration, the matrix <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> would be replaced by the Hessians <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}=\nabla^{2}f(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}=\nabla^{2}f(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, or some approximation thereof for quasi-Newton methods. The variable preconditioning in the proximal precludes the direct use of a single denoiser to replace the proximal step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, the authors consider both static and dynamic preconditioners, with applications to MRI reconstruction. For the dynamic preconditioning, convergence of the variable proximal scaling assumes a “normalization-equivariant denoiser” <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, which is assumed to have the property that for any <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\mu x+\Delta\mathbf{1})=\mu D(x)+\Delta\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\mu x+\Delta\mathbf{1})=\mu D(x)+\Delta\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The idea of adding an adjustable denoiser with diagonal preconditioners to PnP-ADMM is considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, with applications to Poisson denoising.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite> introduces PnP-LBFGS as a method of Newton-type acceleration that entirely bypasses the inclusion of the preconditioner in the proximal step, based on the Minimizing Forward-Backward Envelope (MINFBE) algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite>. They theoretically show superlinear convergence to fixed points of a non-convex functional under standard quasi-Newton assumptions, which leads to significant empirical accelerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The origins of splitting methods can be traced back to the seminal work of Douglas and Rachford (1956) on solving heat conduction problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. Lions and Mercier (1979) later generalized these ideas to maximal monotone operators in Hilbert spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. The modern ADMM framework emerged through the work of Gabay, Mercier (1976) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, and Glowinski (1985) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, with Eckstein (1989) establishing the definitive connection to DRS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. Recent advances have focused on several key directions, for instance, momentum-based variants incorporating Nesterov-type acceleration, stochastic implementations for large-scale problems, nonconvex extensions with convergence guarantees, and distributed implementations for multi-agent systems. The theoretical understanding of these methods continues to deepen, with new connections to differential inclusions and variational inequalities being actively explored.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Learning Image Priors using Denoisers</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will discuss how denoisers can be used to construct an explicit prior, in contrast with proximal PnP schemes where denoisers provide implicit regularization. The main theoretical foundation for designing direct regularizers from Gaussian image denoisers rests on Tweedie’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Tweedie’s Formula</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The use of Gaussian image denoisers as priors can be justified through Tweedie’s formula. Consider the task of estimating the clean image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with a probability density function (p.d.f.) <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> from its noisy measurement <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}=x+\sigma\,w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}=x+\sigma\,w</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise standard deviation and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\text{Id})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>Id</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\text{Id})</annotation></semantics></math>. The p.d.f. of the noisy image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x_{\sigma}|x)=\frac{1}{\left(\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}\right)^{n}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\|x_{\sigma}-x\|_{2}^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x_{\sigma}|x)=\frac{1}{\left(\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}\right)^{n}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\|x_{\sigma}-x\|_{2}^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional density of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> given the clean image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Differentiating <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{\sigma}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{\sigma}|x)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\left(\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}\right)^{n}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\|x_{\sigma}-x\|_{2}^{2}\right)\cdot\left(-\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}(x_{\sigma}-x)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\left(\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}\right)^{n}}\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\|x_{\sigma}-x\|_{2}^{2}\right)\cdot\left(-\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}(x_{\sigma}-x)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}(x_{\sigma}-x)\cdot p(x_{\sigma}|x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}(x_{\sigma}-x)\cdot p(x_{\sigma}|x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Now, differentiating both sides of (<a href="#S3.E26" title="Equation 26 ‣ 3.1 Tweedie’s Formula ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) and using the identity in (<a href="#S3.E27" title="Equation 27 ‣ 3.1 Tweedie’s Formula ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}\left(\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)\right)\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x=-\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}(x_{\sigma}-x)p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}\left(\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)\right)\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x=-\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}(x_{\sigma}-x)p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}\,\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}\,\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, dividing both sides of (<a href="#S3.E28" title="Equation 28 ‣ 3.1 Tweedie’s Formula ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> leads to</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,\,\left(\frac{p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}\right)\,\,\mathrm{d}x." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.340em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,\,\left(\frac{p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}\right)\,\,\mathrm{d}x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}=\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\,p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}=\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}=p(x|x_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{p(x_{\sigma}|x)\,\,p(x)}{p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})}=p(x|x_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>, thanks to Bayes rule, one can write (<a href="#S3.E29" title="Equation 29 ‣ 3.1 Tweedie’s Formula ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S3.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,\,p(x|x_{\sigma})\,\,\mathrm{d}x=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\,\mathbb{E}\left[x|x_{\sigma}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}}x\,\,p(x|x_{\sigma})\,\,\mathrm{d}x=-\frac{x_{\sigma}}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\,\mathbb{E}\left[x|x_{\sigma}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">Rearranging the terms in (<a href="#S3.E30" title="Equation 30 ‣ 3.1 Tweedie’s Formula ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) leads to the familiar Tweedie’s formula:</p>
<table id="S3.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[x|x_{\sigma}\right]-x_{\sigma}=\sigma^{2}\,\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[x|x_{\sigma}\right]-x_{\sigma}=\sigma^{2}\,\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.7" class="ltx_p">which establishes a direct connection between the optimal MMSE Gaussian denoiser and the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">score function</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> of the noisy image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimal Gaussian image denoiser essentially seeks to approximate the conditional mean of the clean image given its noisy observation (as given in (<a href="#S3.E31" title="Equation 31 ‣ 3.1 Tweedie’s Formula ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>)), and hence provides a way for approximating the noisy score function. Such a denoiser is learned by minimizing (an empirical approximation of) the MSE loss <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x,x_{\sigma}}\left\|D(x_{\sigma})-x\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x,x_{\sigma}}\left\|D(x_{\sigma})-x\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on a training dataset having clean and noisy image pairs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Regularization-by-Denoising (RED)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tweedie’s formula is inherently related to the Regularization-by-Denoising (RED) scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, a variant of plug-and-play that leverages a denoiser to approximate the score function. Suppose we are given a denoiser (typically a data-driven one) which approximates the posterior mean <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[x|x_{\sigma}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[x|x_{\sigma}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. One can then construct the RED term by noting the following approximation:</p>
<table id="S3.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}-D(x_{\sigma})\approx-\sigma^{2}\,\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}-D(x_{\sigma})\approx-\sigma^{2}\,\nabla_{x_{\sigma}}\log p_{\sigma}(x_{\sigma}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">i.e. the term <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma}-D(x_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma}-D(x_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> approximates the negative of the score function. This term can be immediately plugged into a gradient step similar to PnP:</p>
<table id="S3.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=x_{k}-\eta[K^{*}(Kx_{k}-y)+\frac{\lambda}{\sigma^{2}}(x_{k}-D(x_{k}))]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=x_{k}-\eta[K^{*}(Kx_{k}-y)+\frac{\lambda}{\sigma^{2}}(x_{k}-D(x_{k}))].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Compared to PnP-PGD, which takes the denoiser as an implicit proximal operator <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(x_{k}-\eta\,K^{*}(Kx_{k}-y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=D_{\sigma}(x_{k}-\eta\,K^{*}(Kx_{k}-y))</annotation></semantics></math>, using <a href="#S3.E33" title="Equation 33 ‣ 3.2 Regularization-by-Denoising (RED) ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a> allows for a more intuitive practical tuning for RED. As reported in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>, proximal PnP’s performance is highly dependent on other algorithmic parameters such as the step size <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> in PnP-PGD. However, for RED, as long as the regularization parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is selected, RED will converge to fixed points with very similar reconstruction quality, as long as the step size <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is below a certain threshold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">RED was first proposed by Romano et al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>. The original motivation was to build an explicit regularization <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)</annotation></semantics></math> using a denoiser function <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)\coloneqq x^{\top}(x-D(x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)\coloneqq x^{\top}(x-D(x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with the hope that the gradient of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is simply <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. To make this true, the denoiser needs to satisfy the local-homogeneity condition:</p>
<table id="S3.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\delta)D(x)=D((1+\delta)x),\quad\text{\,\,for all\,\,}x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\delta)D(x)=D((1+\delta)x),\quad\text{\,\,for all\,\,}x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and sufficiently small <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as symmetry of the denoiser’s Jacobian:</p>
<table id="S3.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="JD(x)^{\top}=JD(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">JD(x)^{\top}=JD(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">However, Reehorst and Schniter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> later clarified that most real-world denoisers do not satisfy the Jacobian symmetry condition; hence, this view of RED is incorrect. The true gradient of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is instead (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, Lem. 2)</cite>):</p>
<table id="S3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla R(x)=x-\frac{1}{2}D(x)-\frac{1}{2}JD(x)^{\top}x" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla R(x)=x-\frac{1}{2}D(x)-\frac{1}{2}JD(x)^{\top}x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has a nonsymmetric Jacobian, which is the case for both non-local filters (e.g., NLM, BM3D, and TNRD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>) and deep denoisers (such as DnCNN). If the denoiser’s Jacobian is not symmetric, then a remarkable result shows that we cannot construct any explicit regularizer whose gradient has exactly the desired form of the denoising residual <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Impossibility of explicit regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, Thm. 1)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the denoiser <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has a asymmetric Jacobian, then there is no regularization <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla R(x)=x-D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla R(x)=x-D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Convergence Theory for RED</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since it is impossible to find an explicit regularizer which exactly satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla R(x)=x-D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla R(x)=x-D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, Reehorst and Schniter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> consider RED’s convergence to a fixed point <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying</p>
<table id="S3.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}(Kx^{*}-y)+\lambda\left(x^{*}-D(x^{*})\right)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}(Kx^{*}-y)+\lambda\left(x^{*}-D(x^{*})\right)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate, we consider a provably convergent RED algorithm, namely proximal gradient RED (RED-PG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>. Given a data fidelity <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> such as the least-squares error <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Kx-y\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Kx-y\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, initialization <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the iterations of RED-PG are described as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{k}=\arg\min_{x}\left\{f(x)+\frac{\lambda L}{2}\|x-v_{k-1}\|^{2}\right\},\\
v_{k}=\frac{1}{L}D(x_{k})-\frac{1-L}{L}x_{k}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{k}=\arg\min_{x}\left\{f(x)+\frac{\lambda L}{2}\|x-v_{k-1}\|^{2}\right\},\\
v_{k}=\frac{1}{L}D(x_{k})-\frac{1-L}{L}x_{k}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(RED-PG)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The basic RED-PG iteration can alternatively be written as iterating the operator <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by:</p>
<table id="S3.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x)\coloneqq\argmin_{z}\left\{f(z)+\frac{\lambda L}{2}\left\|z-\left(\frac{1}{L}D(x)-\frac{1-L}{L}x\right)\right\|^{2}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x)\coloneqq\argmin_{z}\left\{f(z)+\frac{\lambda L}{2}\left\|z-\left(\frac{1}{L}D(x)-\frac{1-L}{L}x\right)\right\|^{2}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">This can be equivalently written using the proximal operator as</p>
<table id="S3.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x)=\prox_{f/(\lambda L)}\left(\frac{1}{L}\big(D(x)-(1-L)x\big)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x)=\prox_{f/(\lambda L)}\left(\frac{1}{L}\big(D(x)-(1-L)x\big)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Using the link between Tweedie’s formula and RED <a href="#S3.E32" title="Equation 32 ‣ 3.2 Regularization-by-Denoising (RED) ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>, the term inside (the argument of the proximal operator in (<a href="#S3.E40" title="Equation 40 ‣ 3.3 Convergence Theory for RED ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>)) may be interpreted as an approximate gradient ascent step on the log-prior, since</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v_{k}=\frac{1}{L}D(x_{k})-\frac{1-L}{L}x_{k}=x_{k}-\frac{1}{L}(x_{k}-D(x_{k}))\approx x_{k}+\frac{1}{L}\nabla\log p_{\sigma}(x_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}=\frac{1}{L}D(x_{k})-\frac{1-L}{L}x_{k}=x_{k}-\frac{1}{L}(x_{k}-D(x_{k}))\approx x_{k}+\frac{1}{L}\nabla\log p_{\sigma}(x_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">which is followed by the proximal step on the data fit <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. For the RED-PG algorithm, it is easy to prove that the operator <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-averaged, that is, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x)=\alpha M(x)+(1-\alpha)T(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x)=\alpha M(x)+(1-\alpha)T(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for some non-expansive operator <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, Lem. 5)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, convex, and continuous; <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-expansive; and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, then the operator <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E39" title="Equation 39 ‣ 3.3 Convergence Theory for RED ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-averaged with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha=\max\left\{\frac{2}{1+L},\frac{2}{3}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha=\max\left\{\frac{2}{1+L},\frac{2}{3}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using this, convergence of RED-PG to the fixed point can be proven for non-expansive denoisers, as stated below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, Thm. 2)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, convex, and continuous; <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-expansive; <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>; and the operator <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E39" title="Equation 39 ‣ 3.3 Convergence Theory for RED ‣ 3 Learning Image Priors using Denoisers ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) has at least one fixed point, then the RED-PG algorithm converges.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Such an approximate gradient descent step can also be accelerated via Nesterov’s momentum as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, Alg. 6)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}x_{k}=\arg\min_{x}\left\{f(x)+\frac{\lambda L}{2}\|x-v_{k-1}\|^{2}\right\},\\
t_{k}=\frac{1+\sqrt{1+4t_{k-1}^{2}}}{2},\\
z_{k}=x_{k}+\frac{t_{k-1}-1}{t_{k}}(x_{k}-x_{k-1}),\\
v_{k}=\frac{1}{L}D(x_{k})-\frac{1-L}{L}z_{k}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}x_{k}=\arg\min_{x}\left\{f(x)+\frac{\lambda L}{2}\|x-v_{k-1}\|^{2}\right\},\\
t_{k}=\frac{1+\sqrt{1+4t_{k-1}^{2}}}{2},\\
z_{k}=x_{k}+\frac{t_{k-1}-1}{t_{k}}(x_{k}-x_{k-1}),\\
v_{k}=\frac{1}{L}D(x_{k})-\frac{1-L}{L}z_{k}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(RED-APG)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly to the lack of convergence results for momentum-based PnP, theoretical convergence of the RED-APG is not yet established and is still an open question.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For stability and mathematical interpretation, it is important to guarantee the convergence of PnP iterations. Since (proximal) PnP methods are derived by replacing proximal operators using off-the-shelf denoisers, their convergence is not automatically guaranteed with a generic denoiser. In this section, we will define some key notions of convergence for PnP methods from weak to strong, and highlight their practical significance. We will also present some recent representative foundational convergence theorems under each category.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Fixed-Point/Iterate Convergence</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is by far the weakest form of convergence, which requires only that the PnP iterations converges to a solution. More formally, the studies on fixed-point convergence consider the PnP iterations as a fixed-point update rule of the form <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k+1}=\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, and seek to determine whether <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to a fixed-point <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The specific structure of the operator <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the choice of the proximal splitting algorithm, the forward operator, and the denoiser. For instance, for PnP-PGD, the iterations are given by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{k+1}=D\left(x_{k}-\eta\,\nabla f(x_{k})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k+1}=D\left(x_{k}-\eta\,\nabla f(x_{k})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}=D\circ\left(\id-\eta\nabla f\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Id</mi><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}=D\circ\left(\id-\eta\nabla f\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that a PnP method is fixed-point convergent if <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique fixed-point <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}(x^{*})=x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}(x^{*})=x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the PnP iterations converge to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{k\rightarrow\infty}{\lim}\,\,x_{k}=x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.507em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{k\rightarrow\infty}{\lim}\,\,x_{k}=x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Achieving fixed-point convergence of a PnP algorithm essentially boils down to ensuring that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a contraction mapping (under suitable conditions on the forward operator and the denoiser). This mode of convergence inherently guarantees that the solution does not worsen as the PnP iterations are repeated a large number of times.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Fixed-point convergence of PnP-DRSdiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, Thm. 4.3)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the PnP-DRSdiff algorithm, given by the iterative updates</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k+\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k+\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\prox_{\tau f}\left(z_{k}\right),\quad x_{k+1}=D\left(2x_{k+\frac{1}{2}}-z_{k}\right),\text{\,\,and\,\,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prox_{\tau f}\left(z_{k}\right),\quad x_{k+1}=D\left(2x_{k+\frac{1}{2}}-z_{k}\right),\text{\,\,and\,\,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=z_{k}+x_{k+1}-x_{k+\frac{1}{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=z_{k}+x_{k+1}-x_{k+\frac{1}{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the data-fidelity term <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex. One can equivalently express (<a href="#S4.E41" title="Equation 41 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Fixed-point convergence of PnP-DRSdiff ( , , Thm. 4.3)) ‣ 4.1 Fixed-Point/Iterate Convergence ‣ 4 Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) as a fixed-point iteration of the form <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{k+1}=\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}(z_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{k+1}=\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}(z_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, with</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}=\frac{1}{2}\id+\frac{1}{2}\left(2D-\id\right)\left(2\,\prox_{\tau f}-\id\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}=\frac{1}{2}\id+\frac{1}{2}\left(2D-\id\right)\left(2\,\prox_{\tau f}-\id\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that the denoiser <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\left(D-\id\right)(u)-\left(D-\id\right)(v)\right\|_{2}\leq\epsilon\left\|u-v\right\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\left(D-\id\right)(u)-\left(D-\id\right)(v)\right\|_{2}\leq\epsilon\left\|u-v\right\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in\mathbb{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in\mathbb{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and some <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the strong convexity parameter <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\epsilon}{(1+\epsilon-2\epsilon^{2})\,\mu}&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\epsilon}{(1+\epsilon-2\epsilon^{2})\,\mu}&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied. Then the operator <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is contractive and the PnP-ADMM algorithm is fixed-point convergent. That is, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(x_{k},z_{k}\right)\rightarrow(x^{*},z^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(x_{k},z_{k}\right)\rightarrow(x^{*},z^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{*},z^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{*},z^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x^{*}=\prox_{\tau f}\left(z^{*}\right)\text{\,\,and\,\,}x^{*}=D\left(2x^{*}-z^{*}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x^{*}=\prox_{\tau f}\left(z^{*}\right)\text{\,\,and\,\,}x^{*}=D\left(2x^{*}-z^{*}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>, fixed-point convergence of PnP-DRSdiff follows from monotone operator theory if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(2D-\id\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(2D-\id\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-expansive, but (<a href="#S4.E43" title="Equation 43 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Fixed-point convergence of PnP-DRSdiff ( , , Thm. 4.3)) ‣ 4.1 Fixed-Point/Iterate Convergence ‣ 4 Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>) imposes a less restrictive condition on the denoiser. Additionally, the data fidelity term is assumed to be strongly convex, which does not hold for ill-posed inverse problems (when the forward operator has a non-trivial null space).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note further that global fixed-point convergence in this sense implies that the reconstruction is independent of the initialization. In practice, the global non-expansiveness does not hold, and instead is softly enforced to hold locally. This allows for convergence from different initializations to different fixed points.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Objective Convergence</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While convergence to a fixed point guarantees stability under repeated iterations, the fixed point generally does not lend itself to a variational interpretation. That is, the fixed point is not generally a minimizer or a stationary point of a variational energy function induced by the denoiser. Objective convergence of PnP draws a direct parallel between PnP and classical variational schemes by ensuring that the PnP solution is indeed a stationary point of some variational objective (which can potentially be non-convex depending on the denoiser). Such convergence can be shown by imposing special structures on the denoiser (while leveraging convergence analysis of proximal gradient descent or some variant of it in the non-convex setting), as we state in the theorem below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Objective convergence of PnP iterations with gradient-step (GS) denoisers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the denoiser is constructed as a gradient-step (GS) denoiser, i.e., <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\id-\nabla g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Id</mi><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\id-\nabla g</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, lower semi-continuous, and differentiable with an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz gradient. The gradient-step PnP algorithm proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\prox_{\tau f}\left(x_{k}-\tau\,\lambda\,\nabla g(x_{k})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prox_{\tau f}\left(x_{k}-\tau\,\lambda\,\nabla g(x_{k})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prox_{\tau f}\circ\left(\tau\lambda\,D+(1-\tau\lambda\,\id)\right)(x_{k})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prox_{\tau f}\circ\left(\tau\lambda\,D+(1-\tau\lambda\,\id)\right)(x_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes data-fidelity and is assumed to be convex and lower semi-continuous. Then, the following guarantees hold for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&lt;\frac{1}{\lambda\,L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&lt;\frac{1}{\lambda\,L}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The sequence </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=f+\lambda\,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=f+\lambda\,g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, is non-increasing and convergent.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\right\|_{2}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\right\|_{2}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, which indicates that iterations are stable, in the sense that they do not diverge if one iterates indefinitely.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All limit points of </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are stationary points of </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notably, the PnP iteration defined by (<a href="#S4.E45" title="Equation 45 ‣ Theorem 4.3 (Objective convergence of PnP iterations with gradient-step (GS) denoisers ( ) ) ‣ 4.2 Objective Convergence ‣ 4 Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>) is exactly equivalent to proximal gradient descent on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+\lambda\,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+\lambda\,g</annotation></semantics></math>, with a potentially non-convex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The construction of gradient-step denoisers is motivated by Tweedie’s formula, which states that the optimal minimum mean squared-error (MMSE) Gaussian denoiser indeed has the form <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)=\id-\nabla g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Id</mi><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)=\id-\nabla g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=\frac{1}{2}\left\|x-N(x)\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=\frac{1}{2}\left\|x-N(x)\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is taken to be the DRUNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite>. As noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, this specific design leads to a powerful denoiser while facilitating convergence analysis. The GS-PnP algorithm and following analysis can be seen to be similar to <a href="#S2.Ex4" title="Equation PnP-PGD ‣ 2.1.3 PnP Proximal Gradient Descent ‣ 2.1 Foundations of Proximal Calculus ‣ 2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">PnP-PGD</span></a>, with the gradient step and proximal step flipped. Some other recent PnP objective convergence results under specific technical assumptions on the denoiser can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Convergent Regularization using PnP</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While objective convergence ensures a one-to-one connection between PnP iterates and the minimization of a variational objective, it does not provide any guarantees about the regularizing properties of the solution that the iterates converge to. In the same spirit as classical regularization theory, it is therefore desirable to be able to control the implicit regularization effected by the denoiser in PnP algorithms and analyze the limiting behavior of the PnP reconstruction as the noise level and the regularization strength tend to zero. More precisely, assuming that the PnP iterations converge to a solution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}\left(y^{\delta},\lambda\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}\left(y^{\delta},\lambda\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the noise level and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an explicit regularization penalty parameter associated with the denoiser, one would like to obtain appropriate selection rules for <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and/or <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}\left(y^{\delta},\lambda\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}\left(y^{\delta},\lambda\right)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits convergence akin to (<a href="#S1.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 1.3 Convergent Regularization ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) in the limit as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>. To the best of our knowledge, one of the first analyses of this kind was reported in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and the precise convergence result is stated in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Convergent PnP regularization ( ) ) ‣ 4.3 Convergent Regularization using PnP ‣ 4 Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Convergent PnP regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the PnP-PGD iterates corresponding to a quadratic fidelity term, which takes the form</span></p>
<table id="S4.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\lambda,k+1}^{\delta}=D_{\lambda}\left(x_{\lambda,k}^{\delta}-\eta\,K^{*}\left(Kx_{\lambda,k}^{\delta}-y^{\delta}\right)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\lambda,k+1}^{\delta}=D_{\lambda}\left(x_{\lambda,k}^{\delta}-\eta\,K^{*}\left(Kx_{\lambda,k}^{\delta}-y^{\delta}\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a denoiser with a tuneable regularization parameter <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PnP\left(\lambda,y^{\delta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PnP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PnP\left(\lambda,y^{\delta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed point of the PnP iteration (<a href="#S4.E46" title="Equation 46 ‣ Theorem 4.4 (Convergent PnP regularization ( ) ) ‣ 4.3 Convergent Regularization using PnP ‣ 4 Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>). For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathscr{R}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathscr{R}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> and any sequence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> of noise levels converging to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a sequence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of regularization parameters converging to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|y_{k}-y^{0}\|_{2}\leq\delta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|y_{k}-y^{0}\|_{2}\leq\delta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following hold under appropriate assumptions on the denoiser (see Definition 3.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> for details):</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PnP\left(\lambda,y^{\delta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PnP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PnP\left(\lambda,y^{\delta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is continuous in </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>δ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The sequence </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\PnP\left(\lambda_{k},y_{k}\right)\right)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>PnP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\PnP\left(\lambda_{k},y_{k}\right)\right)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a weakly convergent subsequence; and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The limit of every weakly convergent subsequence of </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\PnP\left(\lambda_{k},y_{k}\right)\right)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>PnP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\PnP\left(\lambda_{k},y_{k}\right)\right)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a solution of the noiseless operator equation </span><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{0}=Ax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{0}=Ax</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Establishing convergence in the sense of regularization ensures that the implicit regularization effect of the denoiser vanishes to zero as the noise level in the measurement diminishes, thereby guaranteeing that there is no over- or under-regularization. Recently, the convergent regularization property of PnP algorithms with a linear denoiser was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, where the regularization strength of the denoiser is controlled through a spectral filtering-based approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Practical Constraints and Training</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comply with the theoretical analysis, the denoisers used in PnP-like schemes need to satisfy certain constraints. In this section, we mention some of these practical constraints and how they are enforced during training. We also demonstrate the empirical performance of some recent PnP algorithms in terms of image quality and convergence of the iterates.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Weakly Enforced Spectral Constraints</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For convergence analysis, one key requirement is that the denoiser should take the form of a proximal step. For a gradient step denoiser <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma}=I-\nabla g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma}=I-\nabla g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a proximal operator <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma}=\prox_{\phi_{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>prox</mi><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma}=\prox_{\phi_{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math> of some weakly convex function <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, a sufficient condition is that <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz for some <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\sigma}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\sigma}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. Here, we use the subscript <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to denote specifically that the denoiser is trained to remove Gaussian noise of standard deviation <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. As strongly enforcing the Lipschitz condition is difficult, a standard approach in practice is to consider using a soft penalty in the loss function. Noting the equivalence of the Lipschitz constant and the spectral norm of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}g_{\sigma}=J(I-D_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}g_{\sigma}=J(I-D_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>, this consists of adding a spectral regularization term of the form</p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p,\xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})}\max(\|J(I-D_{\sigma})(x+\xi)\|,1-\varepsilon)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p,\xi\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})}\max(\|J(I-D_{\sigma})(x+\xi)\|,1-\varepsilon).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This penalizes the spectral norm of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, typically approximated using a power iteration. Other methods of softly enforcing the Lipschitz constant include (approximate) layer-wise projections onto the Stiefel manifold of orthogonal matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, or doing the same with monotone operators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. However, as the Lipschitz constant is not strictly enforced to be less than one, the algorithms suffer from occasional divergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Backtracking for Lipschitz Control</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Objective convergence of PnP iterations with gradient-step (GS) denoisers ( ) ) ‣ 4.2 Objective Convergence ‣ 4 Convergence of Proximal PnP Methods ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the gradient-step paradigm for PnP instead replaces the gradient step in a splitting with a denoiser, and applies the proximal operator on the fidelity term <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. In this case, since the step size in the splitting is allowed to be variable, it remains to find an upper bound on the Lipschitz constant of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\lambda,\sigma}=I-\lambda\nabla g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\lambda,\sigma}=I-\lambda\nabla g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is a descent step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of approximating the Lipschitz constant to find a (possibly small) appropriate step size, one may instead directly consider the consequential descent condition. This problem takes the following form: find a <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x_{k})-F(\text{GS-PnP}_{\lambda}(x_{k}))\geq\lambda^{-1}\|\text{GS-PnP}_{\lambda}(x_{k})-x_{k}\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>GS-PnP</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>GS-PnP</mtext><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x_{k})-F(\text{GS-PnP}_{\lambda}(x_{k}))\geq\lambda^{-1}\|\text{GS-PnP}_{\lambda}(x_{k})-x_{k}\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This can be executed similarly to an Armijo line search, and can be shown to converge in finitely many iterations under the standard assumptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Property</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another weaker form of convergence utilizes the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz (KL) property. This is a general property that may be satisfied using architectural choices on neural networks. In the following, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the limiting subdifferential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz property <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper and lower semi-continuous function. <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the <em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz (KL) property</em> at a point <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dom\partial^{l}\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dom</mo><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dom\partial^{l}\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta\in(0,+\infty]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,+\infty]</annotation></semantics></math>, a neighbourhood <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and a continuous concave function <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Psi:[0,\eta)\rightarrow[0,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:[0,\eta)\rightarrow[0,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\prime}(s)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\prime}(s)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in(0,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in(0,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u\in U\cap\{\varphi(x_{*})&lt;\varphi(u)&lt;\varphi(x_{*})+\eta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U\cap\{\varphi(x_{*})&lt;\varphi(u)&lt;\varphi(x_{*})+\eta\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{\prime}(\varphi(u)-\varphi(x_{*}))\dist(0,\partial^{l}\varphi(u))\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{\prime}(\varphi(u)-\varphi(x_{*}))\dist(0,\partial^{l}\varphi(u))\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is a KL function if the KL property is satisfied at every point of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dom\partial^{l}\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dom</mo><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dom\partial^{l}\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The KL property can be interpreted as a certain regularity condition. Indeed, if <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the KL property at a critical point <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, then it can be shown that subgradients of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto\Psi\circ(\varphi(u)-\varphi(\overline{u}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto\Psi\circ(\varphi(u)-\varphi(\overline{u}))</annotation></semantics></math> have norm bounded away from one, also known as <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sharpness</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. This geometric property ensures that critical points have sufficient regularity properties. While the definition is seemingly complicated, the KL property holds for many classes of functions, with some examples given in the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following classes of functions satisfy the KL property <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Subanalytic functions that are continuous on their domain (including analytic functions);</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Uniformly convex functions </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for which there exists some </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;0,p\geq 1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;0,p\geq 1,</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that for all </span><math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathcal{X},\,u\in\partial f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathcal{X},\,u\in\partial f(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)\geq f(x)+\langle u,y-x\rangle+K\|y-x\|^{p};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)\geq f(x)+\langle u,y-x\rangle+K\|y-x\|^{p};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semialgebraic functions, which are functions whose graphs are finite unions of the form</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}\mid p_{i}(x)=0,q_{i}(x)&lt;0,i=1,...,p\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}\mid p_{i}(x)=0,q_{i}(x)&lt;0,i=1,...,p\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i},q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i},q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are polynomials.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the following are also semialgebraic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Finite sums and products of semialgebraic functions;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compositions of semialgebraic functions or mappings;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Indicator functions of semialgebraic sets; and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generalized inverses of semialgebraic mappings.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The (sub)analytic functions and semialgebraic characterizations are particularly useful. They imply that smooth neural networks and networks with piecewise-polynomial activations, such as ReLU, both satisfy the KL property, which can be used to show convergence of PnP methods in nonconvex settings. In the absence of convexity, one can use the KL property to show convergence. For example, the following abstract theorem shows convergence under certain conditions on the iterates. Moreover, the convergence is fast in the sense that the sequence has finite length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 2.9)</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper, lower semi-continuous function. Suppose a sequence <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{k})_{k\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{k})_{k\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> and constants <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Sufficient decrease). For </span><math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x_{k+1})+a\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\|^{2}\leq f(x_{k});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x_{k+1})+a\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\|^{2}\leq f(x_{k});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Relative error). For </span><math id="S5.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists </span><math id="S5.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k+1}\in\partial^{l}f(x_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k+1}\in\partial^{l}f(x_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|w_{k+1}\|\leq b\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|w_{k+1}\|\leq b\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Continuity). There exists a subsequence </span><math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{k_{j}})_{j\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{k_{j}})_{j\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and a cluster point </span><math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k_{j}}\rightarrow\tilde{x}\text{ and }f(x_{k_{j}})\rightarrow f(\tilde{x})\quad\text{as }j\rightarrow\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">as </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k_{j}}\rightarrow\tilde{x}\text{ and }f(x_{k_{j}})\rightarrow f(\tilde{x})\quad\text{as }j\rightarrow\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If, furthermore, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has the KL property at the cluster point <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the entire sequence <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{k})_{k\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{k})_{k\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is a critical point of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{k})_{k\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{k})_{k\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> has finite length, i.e., <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\|&lt;+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\|x_{k+1}-x_{k}\|&lt;+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The final property of the above theorem is the most pertinent in the context of PnP algorithms, as it demonstrates convergence to a critical point of the underlying (non-convex) functional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of the properties of some convergent methods. By <span id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iterate convergence</span>, we mean that the entire sequence converges to a point. For methods with residual convergence, they consider convergence of the form <math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{l\leq k}\|x_{l+1}-x_{l}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{l\leq k}\|x_{l+1}-x_{l}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The KL property is used to transform the convergence of residuals to the convergence of iterates. When the denoiser takes the gradient-step form <math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\sigma}=I-\nabla g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\sigma}=I-\nabla g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S5.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> the Lipschitz constant of <math id="S5.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:469.8pt;height:51.8pt;vertical-align:-24.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-259.6pt,28.7pt) scale(0.475037923659341,0.475037923659341) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.6.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">PnP method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Splitting</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Convergent?</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Denoiser constraint</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S5.T1.6.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notion of convergence</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Spectral</th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Line-search</th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">KL</th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Iterate</th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Residual</th>
<th id="S5.T1.6.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Objective</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">DPIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">HQS</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="6">✗</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">PnP-ADMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">ADMM</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" colspan="3">"Bounded denoiser" <math id="S5.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|D_{\sigma}x-x\|^{2}\leq C\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|D_{\sigma}x-x\|^{2}\leq C\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">✗</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">PnP-PGD</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">PGD</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.5.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">✓</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">PnP-<math id="S5.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>PGD</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">PGD</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.6.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">✓</td></tr>
