<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">PnP-DRSdiff</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">DRS</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}&lt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}&lt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✗</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.8.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">✓</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.6.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">PnP-LBFGS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">MINFBE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">✓</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Superlinear</td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>k</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.6.1.9.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">✓</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.3 Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Property ‣ 5 Practical Constraints and Training ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we summarize the properties required for some recent provable PnP methods based on operator splitting convergence. Since the proof structure is quite similar for each of the methods given in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Proximal Splitting Algorithms ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the constraints on the denoisers are also quite similar, and they even converge to critical points of the same functional. This is demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.3 Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Property ‣ 5 Practical Constraints and Training ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where for the deconvolution task with a fixed blur kernel and PnP denoiser, the reconstructions are all fairly similar. Figures <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.3 Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Property ‣ 5 Practical Constraints and Training ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3 Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Property ‣ 5 Practical Constraints and Training ‣ From Image Denoisers to Regularizing Imaging Inverse Problems: An Overview" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrate the residual and peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) convergence for a set of 10 images, again for the image deconvolution task. We observe that, as the theory suggests, the provable PnP methods exhibit decaying residuals and stable PSNR figures, whereas the non-provable DPIR method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite> does not demonstrate such convergence, while deteriorating in quality as the iterations continue.</p>
</div>
